nuvieDefs.h defines `clamp' as a three-argument function-like macro:

    #define clamp(v, c1, c2) ( ((v) < (c1)) ? (c1) : (((v) > (c2)) ? (c2) : (v)) )

libc++ (the OHOS toolchain's C++ standard library) declares std::clamp in
<algorithm>/__algorithm/clamp.h, and one of its overloads takes four
parameters.  A function-like macro hides every later mention of the name from
the preprocessor, so that declaration is macro-expanded instead of parsed:
the invocation then has too many arguments, and the parameter names it was
supposed to declare are gone.  Any translation unit that reaches <algorithm>
transitively therefore fails to compile -- actors/Actor.cpp gets there through
the C++ headers pulled in by U6misc.h / Actor.h:

    .../libcxx-ohos/include/c++/v1/__algorithm/clamp.h:27:57: error: too many
        arguments provided to function-like macro invocation
    .../clamp.h:29:28: error: use of undeclared identifier '__hi'
    .../clamp.h:32:2: error: expected ';' at end of declaration
    make[2]: *** [Makefile:1563: Actor.o] Error 1

A real function collides with nothing, so in C++ mode define `clamp' as one
instead of as a macro.  This is behaviour preserving: every call site in the
tree passes plain ints (clamp(val,-127,dex), clamp(goal.x-loc.x,-1,1),
clamp(atoi(...),0,9), clamp(opacity,0,255), ...), which the macro's conditional
expression already evaluates under the usual arithmetic conversions, i.e. in
int.  C keeps the macro, unchanged.

The only file in the tree with `using namespace std;' is misc/U6misc.cpp, and
it never calls clamp(), so the global function cannot become ambiguous with
std::clamp.

--- a/nuvieDefs.h
+++ b/nuvieDefs.h
@@ -49,7 +49,16 @@
 #define MIN(x, y)      ((x) < (y) ? (x) : (y))
 #define clamp_min(v, c)  (((v) < (c)) ? (c) : (v))
 #define clamp_max(v, c)  (((v) > (c)) ? (c) : (v))
-#define clamp(v, c1, c2) ( ((v) < (c1)) ? (c1) : (((v) > (c2)) ? (c2) : (v)) )
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+/* A three-argument macro named clamp breaks libc++'s <algorithm>, which
+   declares the four-argument std::clamp.  Use a function in C++. */
+static inline int clamp(int v, int c1, int c2)
+{
+    return ((v) < (c1)) ? (c1) : (((v) > (c2)) ? (c2) : (v));
+}
+#else
+#define clamp(v, c1, c2) ( ((v) < (c1)) ? (c1) : (((v) > (c2)) ? (c2) : (v)) )
+#endif

 #define WRAPPED_COORD(c,level) ((c)&((level)?255:1023))
 #define WRAP_COORD(c,level) ((c)&=((level)?255:1023))
